# Data Layer (Prisma + pgvector)

Postgres is the single store of record (memories, embeddings, provenance, audit).

## Rules
- Every domain table carries `tenantId`; every query filters by it. Multi-tenant isolation is non-negotiable.
- Memory `status` lifecycle: `PENDING → ACTIVE → (SUPERSEDED | FORGOTTEN | DELETED)`. Never hard-delete on user request — set `DELETED` + keep an audit tombstone.
- Conflict resolution uses `supersedesId` chains, not in-place overwrites.
- Store `provenance` JSON (source `eventIds`, model, spans) on every memory — this powers governance.
- `embedding` (pgvector) and `keywords` (`tsvector`) are managed via raw SQL in migrations; keep them in sync when content changes.

## Indexing
- Composite indexes on `(tenantId, subjectId, type, status)` and `(tenantId, subjectId, lastUsedAt)`.
- Vector index (IVFFlat/HNSW) chosen at implementation; abstract retrieval so the vector store is swappable.

## Migrations
- Use Prisma migrations; add pgvector/tsvector columns + indexes via the SQL escape hatch.
- Never edit a shipped migration — add a new one.
